WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:05.724
Suppose you want to know, for each user, how many songs they have played.

00:00:05.724 --> 00:00:09.449
You have nine listen rows.

00:00:09.449 --> 00:00:13.173
You want three numbers, one per user.

00:00:13.173 --> 00:00:16.898
The way you ask for that is GROUP BY.

00:00:16.898 --> 00:00:20.622
It takes a long table and squishes it into a short one, where each row is one group.

00:00:20.622 --> 00:00:24.735
The mental model is buckets.

00:00:24.735 --> 00:00:28.849
You name a column.

00:00:28.849 --> 00:00:32.962
Every row that has the same value in that column lands in the same bucket.

00:00:32.962 --> 00:00:37.075
Nine listens, by user, becomes three buckets.

00:00:37.075 --> 00:00:41.188
Mickey, Minnie, and Daffy each have three listens, so every bucket holds three rows, and the output is one row per bucket.

00:00:41.188 --> 00:00:45.302
Pluto, with zero listens, gets no bucket and no row.

00:00:45.302 --> 00:00:49.415
The whole input collapses into three rows, right beside it.

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:52.686
Now, what goes in each bucket?

00:00:52.686 --> 00:00:55.956
You cannot list the rows; you collapsed them.

00:00:55.956 --> 00:00:59.227
So you summarize.

00:00:59.227 --> 00:01:02.498
COUNT counts the rows in each bucket, three each.

00:01:02.498 --> 00:01:05.769
AVG averages the ratings, skipping NULL, so Mickey is four point two.

00:01:05.769 --> 00:01:09.039
MAX takes the highest rating, MIN the lowest.

00:01:09.039 --> 00:01:12.310
Four aggregates, one row per user, all in one pass over the data.

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:16.145
You can group by more than one column, and the bucket key becomes the combination.

00:01:16.145 --> 00:01:19.979
Take Mickey.

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:23.814
By user-ID alone, his three listens are one bucket, count three.

00:01:23.814 --> 00:01:27.648
Add song-ID, and that bucket splits: Mickey song one, Mickey song two, Mickey song six.

00:01:27.648 --> 00:01:31.483
Three buckets, one row each.

00:01:31.483 --> 00:01:35.317
More columns, finer buckets.

00:01:35.317 --> 00:01:39.152
The granularity is whatever combination you name.

00:01:39.152 --> 00:01:43.291
Here is the unsafe query.

00:01:43.291 --> 00:01:47.430
SELECT user-ID, song-ID, COUNT, FROM Listens, GROUP BY user-ID.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:51.569
We grouped by user-ID, so each user collapses to one row, with its count.

00:01:51.569 --> 00:01:55.708
But we also asked for song-ID, and Mickey's group holds three songs: 1, 2, and 6.

00:01:55.708 --> 00:01:59.847
Which one goes in his single row?

00:01:59.847 --> 00:02:03.986
The database can't pick.

00:02:03.986 --> 00:02:08.125
So song-ID is a question mark, an error, or a silent wrong guess.

00:02:08.125 --> 00:02:12.264
Every column you SELECT must be grouped or aggregated.

00:02:12.264 --> 00:02:15.608
The fix needs no second table.

00:02:15.608 --> 00:02:18.952
Just add song-ID to the GROUP BY.

00:02:18.952 --> 00:02:22.295
Now each song is its own group, and every song-ID resolves to exactly one value.

00:02:22.295 --> 00:02:25.639
That is how you ask for it safely.

00:02:25.639 --> 00:02:32.032
GROUP BY is how you turn a list of events into a summary by category.

00:02:32.032 --> 00:02:38.426
It is the engine behind every dashboard, every per-user metric, every "top artists this week" query.

00:02:38.426 --> 00:02:44.819
Pair it with the right aggregate and you can answer almost any "how many" or "on average" question with one query.
